From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:57:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] tests/color-management: Cover ICC file ranges, descriptor 0
 and content

Add regressions for wp_image_description_creator_icc_v1:

 - /wayland/icc-file/wrapping-range: offset UINT32_MAX - 15 with length
   32 wraps to 16 in 32 bits; the client must get the out_of_file
   protocol error on the ICC creator.
 - /wayland/icc-file/past-end: a range one byte past the end of the file
   raises out_of_file.
 - /wayland/icc-file/exact-end: a profile stored after a 7 byte prefix,
   addressed by a range ending exactly at the end of the file, is
   accepted and becomes ready. The client leaves the shared file
   position inside the prefix, so this also checks that the profile is
   read at the absolute offset.
 - /wayland/icc-file/unsupported-content: readable data that is not an
   ICC profile fails with the 'unsupported' cause, not
   'operating_system', and the connection survives. The file position is
   left at the end; since a short read fails as 'operating_system', the
   'unsupported' result shows the whole range was read.
 - /wayland/icc-file/truncated-before-read: the file is valid for the
   range when set_icc_file is processed and truncated before create, the
   point at which the compositor reads it; the short read fails with the
   'operating_system' cause and is never parsed.
 - /wayland/icc-file/zero-descriptor: a stored descriptor 0 counts as set.

The descriptor-0 case cannot be driven through a client, because the
descriptor number the compositor receives is chosen by the kernel on
reception. It is instead a compositor-side unit test of the creator's
file state, using the exported meta_wayland_icc_file_set(): the test
places a memfd on descriptor 0 of the test process, stores it, and checks
that a second set raises already_set while the stored descriptor stays
open and the rejected one is consumed (compared by inode, so descriptor
reuse by another thread cannot fool it). The same test covers the
wrapping, past-end and exact-end ranges directly. It works whether or
not the test process starts with stdin closed (the memfd may itself be
descriptor 0), and restores stdin with a single dup2() over the stored
descriptor, so descriptor 0 is never free for another thread in between.
It replaces the process's descriptor 0, so it runs as a separate
invocation.
---
